{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790441899,"nonce":"S0sc6L9o5Q77YTqIu2jt+Bh/mVc=","title":"AOT runs the JIT's MIR pipeline","message":"AOT lowered each module with only the VM's module passes (3d3513f), so a range loop still called iterate/iteratorValue every iteration, module variables went through memory, and arithmetic on a loop counter stayed boxed.\n\nThe JIT pipeline (RangeLoop, MathGuard, PromoteModuleVars, ConstFold/DCE/CSE, TypeSpecialize, LICM, SRA, UnboxParams, IntSpecialize, LicmModuleVars) now lives in mir::opt::run_native_pipeline, shared by the JIT and AOT. Nothing in it needs deoptimisation once PromoteModuleVars has no loop entries to guard at, which AOT never gives it.\n\nRangeLoop assumes a range ascends. Under AOT it takes the direction into account: constant bounds that ascend are rewritten in place, constant bounds that descend are left generic, and runtime bounds get the counted loop plus a copy that counts down, entered where the count stops when from \u003e to. The JIT keeps its current behaviour (see the miscompile issue).\n\nNative AOT, arm64: `for (i in 0...20000000) s = s + (i * 3 + i - 3) / 2` at top level 0.77s -\u003e 0.129s (JIT 0.12s); the same loop calling a static method 1.36s -\u003e 0.77s. All six bench programs give the same output as the JIT; descending, empty, fractional, nested and negative-bound ranges are covered by aot_runtime and wasm_aot tests.","files":null}]}